@subsection The Core of the Explicit-Control Evaluator

The Kotlin controller is the implementation in
@code{kotlin/@/ch5/@/src/@/main/@/kotlin/@/sicp/@/ch5/@/ExplicitControl.kt}.  It runs on the
shared @code{Machine}; the controller emits the same typed @code{Stmt}
constructors as the compiler.  The evaluator's registers are @code{expr},
@code{env}, @code{val}, @code{proc}, @code{argl}, @code{continue},
@code{kont}, and @code{target}.  @code{expr} carries a checked syntax node,
and @code{kont} carries the explicit continuation-frame chain.

The operation map is built from the checked program by
@code{ExplicitControl.@/operations(checked)}.  The controller dispatches on the
node kind produced by the checked AST, not on source-language lists:

@example kotlin
public fun controller(): List<Stmt> =
    listOf(
        Label("start"),
        Assign("expr",
                OpSrc("entry-expression",
            emptyList())),
        Assign("env",
                OpSrc("make-global-env",
            emptyList())),
        Assign("kont",
                OpSrc("empty-kont",
            emptyList())),
        Assign("continue", LabelSrc("halt")),
        Label("eval-dispatch"),
        Assign("val",
                OpSrc("node-kind",
            listOf(RegSrc("expr")))),
        Test(
            OpCond(
                "kind-is",
                listOf(RegSrc("val"),
                    literal("literal")),
            ),
        ),
        Branch("ev-literal"),
        Test(
            OpCond("kind-is",
                    listOf(RegSrc("val"),
                literal("name"))),
        ),
        Branch("ev-variable"),
        Test(
            OpCond("kind-is",
                    listOf(RegSrc("val"),
                literal("lambda"))),
        ),
        Branch("ev-lambda"),
        Test(
            OpCond("kind-is",
                    listOf(RegSrc("val"),
                literal("if"))),
        ),
        Branch("ev-if"),
        Test(
            OpCond("kind-is",
                    listOf(RegSrc("val"),
                literal("call"))),
        ),
        Branch("ev-call"),
        Test(
            OpCond("kind-is",
                    listOf(RegSrc("val"),
                literal("binary"))),
        ),
        Branch("ev-binary"),
        Test(
            OpCond("kind-is",
                    listOf(RegSrc("val"),
                literal("unary"))),
        ),
        Branch("ev-unary"),
        Test(
            OpCond("kind-is",
                    listOf(RegSrc("val"),
                literal("block"))),
        ),
        Branch("ev-block"),
        Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("ev-structural")),
        Label("ev-literal"),
        Assign(
            "val",
            OpSrc("literal-value",
                listOf(RegSrc("expr"))),
        ),
        Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("continue-dispatch")),
        Label("ev-variable"),
        Assign(
            "val",
            OpSrc("lookup",
                    listOf(RegSrc("expr"),
                RegSrc("env"))),
        ),
        Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("continue-dispatch")),
        Label("ev-lambda"),
        Assign(
            "val",
            OpSrc(
                "make-closure",
                listOf(RegSrc("expr"), RegSrc("env")),
            ),
        ),
        Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("continue-dispatch")),
    )
@end example

@noindent
This is a dispatch excerpt.  The full controller also tests
@code{return}, @code{break}, @code{continue}, @code{local}, @code{assign},
@code{while}, and @code{exprstmt} before its structural fallback.  The
assignment and local cases appear in @ref{5.4.3}; the complete table is in
@code{ExplicitControl.controller}.  Nodes without a direct controller case
reach @code{structural-eval}, which handles members, indexing, @code{When},
loops, templates, and other admitted forms.  Admission rejects unknown or
ill-typed source before the machine runs.

@subsubheading Evaluating conditionals and calls

An @code{If} node pushes its condition, branches, environment, and continuation
into a @code{kont} frame.  A simple call evaluates the callee first; compound
call syntax outside the explicit small-call subset uses the structural
operation:

@example kotlin
listOf(
    Label("ev-if"),
    Assign(
        "kont",
        OpSrc(
            "push-frame",
            listOf(
                literal("if"),
                RegSrc("expr"),
                RegSrc("env"),
                RegSrc("kont"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
    Assign("expr",
            OpSrc("if-condition",
        listOf(RegSrc("expr")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("eval-dispatch")),
    Label("ev-call"),
    Test(OpCond("is-simple-call",
        listOf(RegSrc("expr")))),
    Branch("ev-call-simple"),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("ev-structural")),
    Label("ev-call-simple"),
    Assign(
        "kont",
        OpSrc(
            "push-frame",
            listOf(
                literal("call-fn"),
                RegSrc("expr"),
                RegSrc("env"),
                RegSrc("kont"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
    Assign("expr",
            OpSrc("callee",
        listOf(RegSrc("expr")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("eval-dispatch")),
)
@end example

@noindent
After the arguments are evaluated, @code{fr-apply} sends guest primitive
values through @code{apply-primitive} and guest closures through
@code{extend-environment} and @code{procedure-body}.  These are the
operation names registered by @code{ExplicitControl.operations}; they are not
the original controller's syntax predicates:

@example kotlin
listOf(
    Label("fr-apply"),
    Assign("argl",
            OpSrc("frame-argl",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Assign("kont",
            OpSrc("pop-frame",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Assign("kont",
            OpSrc("push-unwrap",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Test(OpCond("is-primitive",
        listOf(RegSrc("proc")))),
    Branch("fr-apply-primitive"),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("fr-apply-compound")),
    Label("fr-apply-primitive"),
    Assign(
        "val",
        OpSrc(
            "apply-primitive",
            listOf(RegSrc("proc"), RegSrc("argl")),
        ),
    ),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("continue-dispatch")),
    Label("fr-apply-compound"),
    Assign(
        "env",
        OpSrc(
            "extend-environment",
            listOf(RegSrc("proc"), RegSrc("argl")),
        ),
    ),
    Assign("expr",
            OpSrc("procedure-body",
        listOf(RegSrc("proc")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("eval-dispatch")),
)
@end example

@noindent
The explicit continuation is data in @code{kont}; the host language does not
recursively call the evaluator for each guest subexpression.  The machine
stack still monitors actual @code{Save} and @code{Restore} statements.
@node	5.4.2, 5.4.3, 5.4.1, 5.4
@subsection Sequence Evaluation and Tail Recursion

Guest statement sequences are stored in the continuation data in @code{kont}.
Entering a typed @code{Block} pushes a sequence frame; the controller then
evaluates each statement and advances that frame:

@example kotlin
listOf(
    Label("ev-block"),
    Assign(
        "kont",
        OpSrc(
            "push-seq",
            listOf(RegSrc("expr"),
                RegSrc("env"),
                RegSrc("kont")),
        ),
    ),
    Assign("val", OpSrc("unit-value", emptyList())),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("continue-dispatch")),
)
@end example

@noindent
The actual sequence continuation handles the next statement or removes the
frame when the block is complete:

@example kotlin
listOf(
    Label("fr-seq"),
    Test(OpCond("frame-seq-empty",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Branch("fr-seq-done"),
    Assign("expr",
            OpSrc("frame-seq-next",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Assign("kont",
            OpSrc("frame-seq-advance",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("eval-dispatch")),
    Label("fr-seq-done"),
    Assign("kont",
            OpSrc("pop-frame",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("continue-dispatch")),
)
@end example

@noindent
These operation names and labels are the ones in
@code{ExplicitControl.controller} and its operation table.  The continuation
frames are guest data held in @code{kont}; they are not pushes on
@code{Machine.stack}.

@subsubheading Tail recursion

A guest call may be in tail position even when the controller does not
eliminate its continuation data.  For example:

@example kotlin
tailrec fun countDown(n: Long): Long @{
    if (n == 0L) return 0L
    return countDown(n - 1L)
@}

fun main() @{
    println(countDown(10L))
@}
@end example

The Kotlin @code{tailrec} annotation constrains the checked guest function,
but the explicit-control engine does not apply the Kotlin compiler's native
tail-call transformation.  Calls use @code{kont} frames, and the current
controller has no tail-call frame-reuse path.  Therefore tail position alone
does not imply constant total continuation storage.

@code{Machine.stack} counts @code{Save} and @code{Restore} instructions; it
does not count the evaluator's @code{kont} chain.  Any measurement of tail
recursion must distinguish machine-stack counters from continuation-frame
growth.  Exercises 5.26-5.29 ask for those observations rather than assuming
the host language's tail-call behavior.

@node	5.4.3, 5.4.4, 5.4.2, 5.4
@subsection Conditionals, Assignments, and Definitions

The controller implements @code{If}, local declarations, and assignments to
name targets with explicit continuation frames.  Other typed forms are passed
to @code{structural-eval}; the explicit-control evaluator is a deliberate
subset, not a second implementation of every guest-language form.

For an @code{If}, @code{ev-if} saves the expression and environment in an
@code{if} frame, then evaluates its condition:

@example kotlin
listOf(
    Label("ev-if"),
    Assign(
        "kont",
        OpSrc("push-frame",
                listOf(literal("if"),
                RegSrc("expr"),
            RegSrc("env"), RegSrc("kont"))),
    ),
    Assign("expr",
            OpSrc("if-condition",
        listOf(RegSrc("expr")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("eval-dispatch")),
)
@end example

@noindent
When the condition value returns, @code{fr-if} selects the branch, restores
the saved environment, removes the frame, and dispatches the selected
expression:

@example kotlin
listOf(
    Label("fr-if"),
    Test(OpCond("is-true", listOf(RegSrc("val")))),
    Branch("fr-if-yes"),
    Assign("expr",
            OpSrc("frame-else",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Assign("env",
            OpSrc("frame-env",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Assign("kont",
            OpSrc("pop-frame",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("eval-dispatch")),
    Label("fr-if-yes"),
    Assign("expr",
            OpSrc("frame-then",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Assign("env",
            OpSrc("frame-env",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Assign("kont",
            OpSrc("pop-frame",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("eval-dispatch")),
)
@end example

@subsubheading Assignments and definitions

The direct assignment case is restricted to a name target.  Other assignment
targets use @code{structural-eval}.  The direct case evaluates the right-hand
side under an @code{assign} continuation, whose handler updates the environment:

@example kotlin
listOf(
    Label("ev-assign"),
    Test(OpCond("is-name-target",
        listOf(RegSrc("expr")))),
    Branch("ev-assign-name"),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("ev-structural")),
    Label("ev-assign-name"),
    Assign(
        "kont",
        OpSrc("push-frame",
                listOf(literal("assign"),
                RegSrc("expr"),
            RegSrc("env"), RegSrc("kont"))),
    ),
    Assign("expr",
            OpSrc("assign-value",
        listOf(RegSrc("expr")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("eval-dispatch")),
    Label("fr-assign"),
    Assign(
        "val",
        OpSrc("do-assign",
                listOf(RegSrc("kont"),
            RegSrc("val"))),
    ),
    Assign("kont",
            OpSrc("pop-frame",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("continue-dispatch")),
)
@end example

Local declarations also use a continuation frame while evaluating the
initializer:

@example kotlin
listOf(
    Label("ev-local"),
    Assign(
        "kont",
        OpSrc("push-frame",
                listOf(literal("local"),
                RegSrc("expr"),
            RegSrc("env"), RegSrc("kont"))),
    ),
    Assign("expr",
            OpSrc("initializer",
        listOf(RegSrc("expr")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("eval-dispatch")),
    Label("fr-local"),
    Assign(
        "env",
        OpSrc(
            "declare-local",
            listOf(RegSrc("kont"), RegSrc("val")),
        ),
    ),
    Assign("val", OpSrc("unit-value", emptyList())),
    Assign("kont",
            OpSrc("pop-frame",
        listOf(RegSrc("kont")))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("continue-dispatch")),
)
@end example

@noindent
Function declarations and other forms not listed in the controller dispatch
fall through to @code{structural-eval}; this controller has no separate
@code{ev-definition} label or @code{define-variable!} operation.

