@subsection Running the Evaluator

With the implementation of the explicit-control evaluator we come to the end of
a development, begun in @ref{Chapter 1}, in which we have explored successively
more precise models of the evaluation process.  We started with the relatively
informal substitution model, then extended this in @ref{Chapter 3} to the
environment model, which enabled us to deal with state and change.  In the
metacircular evaluator of @ref{Chapter 4}, we used guest Kotlin as a language
for making more explicit the environment structure constructed during
evaluation of an expression.  Now, with register machines, we have taken a
close look at the evaluator's mechanisms for storage management, argument
passing, and control.  At each new level of description, we have had to raise
issues and resolve ambiguities that were not apparent at the previous, less
precise treatment of evaluation.  To understand the behavior of the
explicit-control evaluator, we can simulate it and monitor its performance.

The evaluator interface in this edition is a host API, not a simulated
read-eval-print loop.  Admit one complete guest program, then run its checked
form:

@example kotlin
val source = "fun main() @{ println(1L) @}"
val result = ExplicitControl.run(source, Mode.CORE)
@end example

The source overload admits the program and returns
@code{Either<AdmissionError, RunResult>}; @code{run(checked)} returns the
@code{RunResult} directly.  Its @code{output}, @code{error}, and
@code{mainValue} fields contain captured output, any typed runtime error, and
the completion value.  The engine does not install @code{read},
@code{prompt-for-input}, or other REPL operations in its controller.

Here is a complete guest program that appends and renders lists:

@example kotlin
fun append(
    xs: List<String>,
    ys: List<String>,
): List<String> = xs + ys

fun show(items: List<String>): String =
    if (items.isEmpty()) "" else if (items.size == 1) items.get(0)
    else items.get(0) + " " + show(items.drop(1))

fun main() @{
    val result = append(
        listOf("a", "b", "c"),
        listOf("d", "e", "f"),
    )
    println("(" + show(result) + ")")
@}
@end example

@noindent
Use @code{ExplicitControl.machine(checked)} when you need access to the
machine's monitored stack and instruction counter.  The sample below reads
those counters after one run:

@example kotlin
val checked = Admission.admit(source, Mode.CORE).fold(
    @{ admissionError -> kotlin.error(admissionError.category) @},
    @{ it @},
)
val machine = ExplicitControl.machine(checked)
either @{ machine.run() @}
val pushes = machine.stack.pushes
val depth = machine.stack.maxDepth
val instructions = machine.instructions
@end example

@noindent
The monitored @code{Machine.stack} counts only controller @code{Save} and
@code{Restore} instructions.  It does not count the explicit continuation
frames linked through @code{kont}.  The machine exposes @code{kont} through
@code{Machine.registers}; each continuation frame is a @code{GValue.VObject}
whose @code{rest} field links to the next frame.  This host-side meter samples
the chain after each machine step.  It measures logical depth, not replacement
frame allocations; use it on a fresh machine instead of calling @code{run}:

@example kotlin
private fun kontDepth(start: GValue): Int @{
    var current = start
    var depth = 0
    while (current is GValue.VObject &&
        current.className == "frame") @{
        depth++
        current = current.fields["rest"] ?: GValue.VNull
    @}
    return depth
@}

context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
private fun maxKontDepth(machine: Machine): Int @{
    var maximum = 0
    while (!machine.halted()) @{
        machine.step()
        val kont = machine.registers.getValue("kont").content
        maximum = maxOf(maximum, kontDepth(kont))
    @}
    return maximum
@}
@end example

Call @code{maxKontDepth} inside the guest-error scope used for @code{step};
the function consumes the machine to halt.  Read stack and instruction
counters from that same machine afterward.  Reading @code{kont} only after
halt returns zero because the active frames have been removed.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.26}Exercise 5.26:} Measure the tail-recursive
factorial below with @code{ExplicitControl.machine(checked)}:

@example kotlin
fun factorial(n: Long): Long @{
    fun iter(product: Long, counter: Long): Long =
        if (counter > n) product
        else iter(product * counter, counter + 1L)
    return iter(1L, 1L)
@}

fun main() @{
    println(factorial(5L))
@}
@end example

For each small @math{n}, record @code{Machine.stack.maxDepth},
@code{Machine.stack.pushes}, instruction count, and maximum @code{kont} frame
depth.  The controller does not reuse continuation frames for tail calls, so
do not assume that the guest function's tail position gives constant
continuation space.  Explain which counters measure machine-stack use and
which measure evaluator continuation space.
@end quotation

@quotation
